       0.1::double precision + 0.2::double precision AS estimate,
       0.1::numeric + 0.2::numeric AS exact_sum;
-- stored_fee | estimate            | exact_sum
-- 20.01      | 0.30000000000000004 | 0.3`;
const clock = `SET TIME ZONE 'UTC';
SELECT timestamptz '2026-10-25 00:30+00' AS confirmed_at;
-- 2026-10-25 00:30:00+00

SET TIME ZONE 'Europe/London';
SELECT timestamptz '2026-10-25 00:30+00' AS confirmed_at;
-- 2026-10-25 01:30:00+01`;
const defaults = `CREATE TEMP TABLE contact_example (
  id integer,
  phone_state text DEFAULT 'unprovided'
);
INSERT INTO contact_example (id) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO contact_example VALUES (2, NULL);
SELECT * FROM contact_example ORDER BY id;
-- 1 | unprovided
-- 2 | NULL`;
const json = `SELECT '{}'::jsonb -> 'phone' IS NULL AS absent,
       '{"phone":null}'::jsonb -> 'phone' IS NULL AS json_null,
       NULL::jsonb IS NULL AS sql_null;
-- absent | json_null | sql_null
-- true   | false     | true`;
---
<p>A column type decides which values a database can store and which operations it understands. It cannot decide what the value means. A fee of <code>20</code> can be stored perfectly while losing whether it means euros or cents. A timestamp can preserve every digit while losing which occurrence of a repeated clock time the customer booked.</p>
<p>We will extend the class-booking service from <a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing relational data</a>. Mina’s booking has an agreed fee, a membership code, a session start and optional contact details. For each field, the question is what must survive from input, through storage, to the next reader. PostgreSQL 18 supplies the concrete examples; selected differences matter when the same declaration would preserve something different elsewhere.</p>
