<h2 id="start-with-what-a-value-must-preserve">Start with what a value must preserve</h2>
<p>Write a small <strong>representation contract</strong> before choosing a type: the meaning and unit, allowed range, distinctions that must remain, equality and ordering, and operations the application needs. Then choose the database type and application representation together.</p>
<table><caption>Four booking fields, before choosing their storage</caption><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What must survive</th><th>Useful operations</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Agreed fee</td><td>Whole euro cents; 0–999,999.99; known, finite amount</td><td>Sum fees in the same currency; compare agreements</td></tr>
<tr><td>Membership code</td><td>Exact spelling within a tenant, including leading zeroes</td><td>Exact lookup; detect a duplicate code</td></tr>
<tr><td>Session start</td><td>One resolved instant</td><td>Order occurrences; display in the venue’s zone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contact phone</td><td>A supplied string or no number recorded</td><td>Display or contact; no numeric arithmetic</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The domain is the set of meaningful values, not merely everything the type accepts. A PostgreSQL decimal column can accept a negative fee or <code>NaN</code>; a timestamp can accept infinity. Our booking contract permits neither. <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/">Constraints</a> makes requiredness and allowed states enforceable. Here we choose the representations those rules act on.</p>
<p>Keep display separate. A formatted fee such as <code>€1,234.50</code> is useful on a screen; storing that as the amount makes arithmetic depend on parsing punctuation and locale. Store the amount and its currency meaning, then format for the reader. Likewise, “Tuesday evening” is a label, not a substitute for a scheduled occurrence.</p>

<h2 id="choose-numeric-representations-from-the-error-you-can-accept">Choose numeric representations from the error you can accept</h2>
<p>An integer represents whole units exactly within a fixed range. In PostgreSQL, <code>integer</code> stops at 2,147,483,647; <code>bigint</code> stops at 9,223,372,036,854,775,807. Exceeding the range is an error. Pick the range for the lifetime and largest intermediate values of the field, not the small numbers in a sample.</p>
<p>For our fees, <code>numeric(8,2)</code> gives eight decimal digits in total, two after the decimal point: up to 999,999.99 in magnitude. Precision is the total digit capacity; scale is the fractional digit count. Exactness means a representable decimal value is stored exactly. It does not mean every submitted value fits without change.</p>
<p>Figure 1 starts with €20.00. Choose <em>20.005</em>, step through its assignment, then change the input policy at the final step.</p>
<RepresentationFigure kind="number" />
<p>PostgreSQL rounds an excess fraction to the declared scale before checking the remaining range. Thus €20.005 becomes €20.01; €999,999.995 rounds to €1,000,000.00 and overflows. The parent’s whole-cent contract requires detecting a fractional cent before assignment. A check on the rounded stored value cannot recover that discarded digit.</p>
<p>Integer minor units are another exact representation: <code>2000</code> cents means €20.00. They remove fractional storage for this field, but they move the scale into the contract. A service accepting several currencies must retain the currency and its permitted unit scale. “Every amount has two decimal places” is not a safe multi-currency design. Measured quantities and prorated charges may also need finer units than the final payable amount.</p>
<p>Exact storage does not settle calculation policy. Split one euro equally between three bookings and there is no exact whole-cent share. Decide where rounding happens and how the remaining cent is allocated. Rounding each line before summing can differ from rounding the final total. Record the calculation rule as well as the column declaration; <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/">Getting SQL values right</a> covers arithmetic, casts and expression types.</p>
<p><strong>Floating point</strong> represents a wide range with limited significant digits and approximate values. It suits measured temperatures or estimated attendance rates when an explicit error tolerance is acceptable. It is a poor default for agreed decimal fees. In the executed PostgreSQL example, two short decimal-looking inputs produce a visible approximation:</p>
<Sql code={numeric} label="Exact decimal storage and approximate floating-point arithmetic" />
<p>Neither a neat display nor a fixed number of printed decimal places establishes exactness. Also inspect the route into and out of storage. A correct <code>bigint</code> can lose an identifier digit when application code converts it to a JavaScript <code>Number</code>. We will test that boundary at the end.</p>

<h2 id="preserve-identity-and-define-text-equality">Preserve identity and define text equality</h2>
<p>A string containing digits is not necessarily a quantity. Membership code <code>007</code> and code <code>7</code> may identify different customers. Converting both to integers destroys that distinction. Phone numbers have prefixes and formatting rules, not meaningful multiplication or addition. Keep them as text with a defined accepted form.</p>
<p>An integer internal identifier is compact and exact within its range. A native UUID type represents a 128-bit identifier and lets the engine validate UUID input; PostgreSQL outputs a standard spelling even when it accepts another spelling. Text preserves an external identifier’s alphabet, prefixes and leading zeroes. Retain the provider or tenant namespace where the same text can be reused. The parent owns how identities are allocated and scoped; a storage type alone supplies neither uniqueness nor authority.</p>
<p>Text needs several decisions that are easy to confuse. Preserve Mina’s original name for display. Choose a locale-aware order for a roster. Define search matching, which might ignore accents. Define identity equality for a membership code. A search that finds “Jose” when someone types “José” does not establish that two customers are the same person.</p>
<p>Encoding determines how characters become bytes. Unicode normalisation can give a common representation to canonically equivalent spellings, such as <code>é</code> and <code>e</code> followed by a combining acute accent. It does not decide whether to ignore case, accents or punctuation. Choose those policies for the field and its users. Store an original spelling and a separate matching form when both are needed; every writer must maintain that relationship.</p>
<p>Figure 2 uses the deliberately small ASCII membership-code alphabet. Compare a lower-case letter and a trailing space under exact matching and an explicit canonical form.</p>
<RepresentationFigure kind="text" />
<p>A <strong>collation</strong> supplies text comparison and ordering rules. In PostgreSQL, a deterministic collation breaks comparison ties by bytes; a deliberately configured nondeterministic collation can treat different case, accents or normal forms as equal. Do not assume a human-friendly sort order changes equality in the way you intend. Check the actual provider and configuration.</p>
<table><caption>A trailing space can change which codes count as duplicates</caption><thead><tr><th>Selected engine and type</th><th>Does a trailing space matter in equality?</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>PostgreSQL 18 <code>text</code>, <code>C</code> collation</td><td>Yes: <code>'M007'</code> differs from <code>'M007 '</code></td><td>Reject or preserve spaces deliberately</td></tr>
<tr><td>PostgreSQL 18 <code>char(n)</code></td><td>Trailing padding is ignored between these values</td><td>A fixed-width declaration changes the equality contract</td></tr>
<tr><td>MySQL 8.4 <code>VARCHAR</code></td><td>Depends on collation: <code>PAD SPACE</code> ignores trailing spaces; <code>NO PAD</code> treats them as significant</td><td>Check the collation’s pad attribute before moving code data</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The PostgreSQL comparisons were executed; the MySQL comparison follows its manual. <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/#close-the-gaps-in-required-values-and-identity">Uniqueness enforcement</a> must use the chosen equality rule. Applying a different rule only in a form leaves imports and concurrent writes free to disagree.</p>
<p>Length needs a unit too. PostgreSQL’s <code>varchar(n)</code> limits characters rather than bytes, but a visible glyph can contain several Unicode code points. In our UTF-8 check, precomposed <code>é</code> was one character and two bytes. Choose limits from a real input, protocol or storage requirement; do not assume an arbitrary length of 255 is a performance optimisation. PostgreSQL documents no speed advantage for <code>char(n)</code> over ordinary text.</p>

<h2 id="store-the-time-the-application-actually-means">Store the time the application actually means</h2>
<p>A calendar date, a wall-clock reading, an instant and an elapsed duration answer different questions. A booking’s intended day can be a <code>date</code>. Venue opening time can be a local <code>time</code>. A confirmation event and a resolved session occurrence need instants. “Hold this reservation for 900 seconds” is an elapsed duration, not a calendar appointment.</p>
<p>A local timestamp says what the clock and calendar read. To resolve it to an instant, the application needs a zone or an explicit offset. An offset such as <code>+01:00</code> fixes the UTC difference for one occurrence. A named zone such as <code>Europe/London</code> supplies rules that vary with date and can change when authorities revise them. One offset cannot describe a recurring local schedule.</p>
<p>Figure 3 follows one elapsed hour across London’s autumn clock change. Step to the end with <em>Clock reading only</em>, then choose <em>Instant with offset</em>.</p>
<RepresentationFigure kind="time" />
<p>London reads 01:30 twice on 25 October 2026. The explicit values <code>01:30+01:00</code> and <code>01:30+00:00</code> identify different instants. In spring, 01:30 on 29 March does not occur on London’s local clock at all. A service must choose whether to reject such local input, request clarification, skip an occurrence or shift it according to a stated rule. An engine’s automatic resolution is not a business policy.</p>
<p>For example, PostgreSQL resolves the ambiguous London input <code>2026-10-25 01:30</code> with the standard-time offset; our native run produced 01:30 UTC. It also accepts the nonexistent spring reading by applying its documented transition rule. If our booking policy requires clarification, permitting that conversion silently would violate it.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL’s <code>timestamptz</code> preserves an instant and renders it in the session’s time zone. It retains neither the original named zone nor the original offset. These two displays identify the same instant:</p>
<Sql code={clock} label="Changing the session zone changes display, not the stored instant" />
<p>For “every Tuesday at 18:00 in Europe/London”, retain the weekday, local time and named zone, plus the rule for generating occurrences. Resolve individual session starts into instants. Decide whether already advertised occurrences stay fixed when time-zone rules change or are recalculated to preserve local time. Those are promises to attendees, not formatting options. <a href="/review/relational-next/history/">History modelling</a> handles retaining earlier agreements when that becomes necessary.</p>
<p>Choose precision at interchange too. PostgreSQL timestamps can preserve microseconds; JavaScript <code>Date</code> represents milliseconds. Our later driver check loses the last three fractional digits unless another representation is chosen. For elapsed durations, define the unit and range explicitly, for example integer seconds. PostgreSQL intervals can contain months and days as well as time, so “one month” must not be treated as a fixed number of elapsed seconds.</p>

<h2 id="represent-missing-information-without-inventing-facts">Represent missing information without inventing facts</h2>
<p><code>NULL</code> records the absence of an SQL value. Its business meaning belongs in the contract. In the parent schema, a nullable phone means “no number recorded”. That is sufficient while the application makes no distinction between the reasons.</p>
<p>An empty string is a present text value of length zero. An empty list contains no elements. A zero fee is a known amount. No related booking rows means the customer has no bookings. None of those facts means “we do not know”. A sentinel such as 1 January 1900 turns “unknown date” into an apparently real date that can slip into reports and ordering.</p>
<p>Now staff must avoid asking again when a customer has declined to provide a phone. The distinction between unprovided and declined has become useful information. Figure 4 shows what a nullable phone can and cannot retain.</p>
<RepresentationFigure kind="missing" />
<p>Use an explicit state, such as <code>unprovided</code>, <code>declined</code> or <code>provided</code>, with the associated phone. A provided state requires a valid phone; the other two require no phone. A state without those combination rules merely creates new contradictory records. <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/">Constraints</a> owns enforcing the valid combinations. An inapplicable field may need another state, but only if the service needs to retain that reason.</p>
<p>A <strong>default</strong> answers what to store when an insert omits a field or explicitly requests <code>DEFAULT</code>. It does not generally replace an explicit null. Here is PostgreSQL’s behaviour, with constraints deliberately left out so both outcomes remain visible:</p>
<Sql code={defaults} label="Omitted input and explicit NULL are different inputs" />
<p>That default asserts that an omitted state means unprovided. Choose it only if every caller is allowed to make that assertion. A <code>NOT NULL</code> rule would reject the second insert; it would not turn it into the default. Partial-update APIs also need to distinguish “leave unchanged” from “clear this value”. Preserve that distinction before constructing the SQL.</p>
<p>Missing values also affect comparisons and uniqueness. <a href="/review/relational-next/sql-values/">Getting SQL values right</a> explains SQL’s unknown expression result and <code>IS NULL</code>; <a href="/review/relational-next/constraints/">constraints</a> checks the selected engine’s null uniqueness rules. Choose the meaning here, then verify that the operations implement it.</p>

<h2 id="choose-the-boundary-of-a-structured-value">Choose the boundary of a structured value</h2>
<p>Ordinary columns expose separate values to types, predicates and constraints. Related rows expose independently identified facts and relationships. An array or JSON value can keep a small structure together when the enclosing booking owns it as a whole. The useful boundary follows validation, queries and changes.</p>
<p>An array suits a homogeneous sequence, such as a few ordered instructions, when the engine supports the operations needed. It does not automatically make each element a referenced row, remove duplicates or give elements stable identities. JSON accommodates named fields and varied shapes. Syntactically valid JSON still needs a shape contract: permitted fields, value types, missing-field meanings and accepted versions.</p>
<p>Suppose an optional booking payload starts as two notes printed on Mina’s confirmation. Figure 5 changes the requirement while keeping the proposed representation visible.</p>
<RepresentationFigure kind="structure" />
<p>If every option must reference an existing catalogue item, related rows give that reference a normal foreign key. If staff fulfil each option separately, an option row can have a stable identifier, status and update boundary. JSON can still carry supplementary provider details, but those requirements should not be hidden inside an undocumented object. Frequent filtering, per-element uniqueness and independent ownership are further reasons to expose rows or columns. <a href="/review/relational-next/normalisation/">Normalisation</a> develops the dependencies; this choice starts with what must be independently meaningful.</p>
<p>JSON also has its own missing distinctions. A missing <code>phone</code> property, a present property whose value is JSON <code>null</code>, and a SQL-null JSON column are three different representations. PostgreSQL’s JSON extraction illustrates the distinction:</p>
<Sql code={json} label="A missing JSON property is different from a JSON null value" />
<p>A particular extraction or application mapping can collapse them later, so test the operation you use. For retained JSON, define how an older payload is read: for example version 1 accepts a missing notes field as an empty list, while a null notes field is invalid. A newer reader must either support the old shape or reject it explicitly. Adding a property to today’s writer does not update yesterday’s rows.</p>
<p>Binary values, such as a small signed confirmation, have another boundary. A database binary column keeps bytes with the row’s transaction and access rules, but does not validate the PDF’s business meaning. Large payloads can increase backup, replication and transfer costs; <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">storage</a> explains where wide values live. An external payload reference keeps the database row small, but needs an immutable object identity, size or checksum where useful, and an access and deletion policy.</p>
<p>A database transaction cannot by itself make an independent object-store upload and a row commit atomic. The object can exist without the row, or the row can point to an absent object. Choose and test a publication and cleanup protocol; <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">application integration</a> owns coordination and reconciliation, and <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">restoration</a> must recover matching database and payload copies.</p>

<h2 id="check-representations-at-the-application-boundary">Check representations at the application boundary</h2>
<p>A successful insert proves that the database accepted a value. A useful fixture also proves which inputs are refused, what storage changed, and what the application returns after reading and serialising it. Use boundary values rather than only convenient examples.</p>
<table><caption>Fixtures for this booking design, and what they expose</caption><thead><tr><th>Fixture</th><th>Expected contract</th><th>Observed check</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>2147483648</code> as PostgreSQL <code>integer</code></td><td>Refuse overflow</td><td>Native PostgreSQL rejected it</td></tr>
<tr><td>€20.005 into <code>numeric(8,2)</code></td><td>Whole-cent input policy must reject before assignment</td><td>Database assignment alone stored 20.01</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>9007199254740993</code> as <code>bigint</code></td><td>Preserve every identifier digit</td><td>PGlite returned a JavaScript BigInt; conversion to Number changed it to 9007199254740992</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>007</code>; code case and trailing space</td><td>Preserve text and chosen equality</td><td>Leading zeroes survived; native exact comparisons distinguished the variants</td></tr>
<tr><td>Precomposed and decomposed <code>é</code></td><td>Match only under the chosen policy</td><td>Native raw equality differed; NFC-normalised equality matched</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two London 01:30 occurrences</td><td>Retain resolved instants</td><td>Native zone conversion gave the same wall-clock reading for two UTC instants</td></tr>
<tr><td>Null, empty text and omitted input</td><td>Keep their meanings separate</td><td>Native default and length/null checks distinguished them</td></tr>
<tr><td>Microsecond instant</td><td>Retain only the precision the application promises</td><td>PGlite’s Date result changed .123456 to .123</td></tr>
<tr><td>Older JSON <code>{'{}'}</code> and <code>{'{"phone":null}'}</code></td><td>Distinguish absence from explicit JSON null</td><td>PGlite objects distinguished missing property from present null</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The native SQL ran in a disposable PostgreSQL 18.6 database with UTF-8, <code>C</code> locale and UTC except for the explicit session-zone comparison. The application checks used PGlite 0.5.8, a PostgreSQL 18.3 WebAssembly build, through its JavaScript query API. These are two recorded runtimes, not one interchangeable measurement. The figures are explanatory models; they do not query either server.</p>
<p>In that application run, the decimal fee arrived as a string and the large integer as a BigInt. Plain <code>JSON.stringify</code> refused the BigInt. Explicitly encoding the identifier as a decimal string preserved it through JSON; coercing it to Number did not. A production driver may choose different mappings, so repeat the fixture against its pinned version and any configured parsers. For timestamps, use an agreed text or integer representation if milliseconds are insufficient, rather than converting through Date first.</p>
<p>Our completed choices are exact whole-cent euro amounts, text membership codes with explicit equality, resolved session instants, and one nullable phone until the service needs distinct reasons for absence. Structured options gain rows when they gain independent references or changes. The remaining work has concrete owners: constraints enforce accepted states, SQL expressions preserve the intended operations, and <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">live schema changes</a> move existing data and callers to a new contract without silently reinterpreting it.</p>

